From 35ad2ee7788d42610999c07baf7ee8e2e5b51818 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 17:37:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][Subtitles] 3D subtitles side by side and top and
 bottom: each eye gets those of the 2D picture, squeezed

In a 3D output side by side, 1.2.2 drew the text subtitles of each eye
four times as wide as they should be, with a quarter of the words on a
line ("4 words per row on my 65 inch"), and Kodi before it twice as wide
with half the words.

Kodi's pixel ratio of a resolution is the width of a pixel over its
height as it is shown: 2 for each eye side by side, whose half of the
width the display stretches back, 0.5 top and bottom. libass's pixel
aspect ratio is the other way round: that of the picture that a script
was made for over the frame that it renders into, and it draws the
glyphs and lays out the lines that much wider (libass 0.17.5: the
outline transform multiplies x by par_scale_x, and a line wraps at the
frame width over par_scale_x). Kodi gave it 2 - ratio, which is
1 / ratio near 1 only (0 side by side, libass's default: square here);
1.2.2 gave it the ratio itself. libass is now given 1 / ratio
(LibassPixelAspect()): measured with the libass of the build, each eye
of 960x1080 and 1920x540 at 1080p, and of 1920x2160 and 3840x1080 at
2160p, gets glyphs of their 2D shape, lines as wide and as many as in
2D and text as tall as in 2D, as the display shows them.

A bitmap subtitle whose canvas has another shape than the video (a
re-encoded or cropped film with its Blu-ray PGS, aligned to the screen
with its aspect ratio) was fitted into the pixels of the eye: a canvas
of 1920x1080 in an eye of 960x1080 was drawn at half its size both ways
and shown at its width but half its height. It is now fitted into the
eye as it is shown and drawn that much narrower or wider
(OverlayShape.h, FitShownScale()). A canvas of the video's shape is
scaled with the video, which was right. In 2D on square pixels nothing
changes.
